speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, on rising to participate in the debate on the resolution proposing the appointment of a special committee to study the drug laws and the dangers of chemical pesticides, may I say that on this side of the house, although we are happy to see the minister finally acquiesce in the pleas of the opposition to refer this whole matter of drugs and pesticides to a special committee, nevertheless we are disappointed that the minister took so long and delayed unnecessarily this decision, which no doubt will prevent patients, drug manufacturers and medical researchers from benefiting earlier from the recommendations which we hope the government will translate into law without further delay for the common good of all in this country.
We regret that the minister fell into the same irresponsible attitude of his cabinet colleagues, the pathological attitude, so characteristic of the present administration, to postpone decisions in important matters. It is quite apparent that this government is paralysed with fear of making important decisions, and most likely for the reason that it fears to face grim reality; afraid that it might make a mistake, as it so often does;

afraid to be criticized; afraid of the tribunal of public scrutiny. In short it is afraid to govern.
